#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278212 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is defined for all $x$ and $y,$ and\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡g(x,y)$ is defined if $g(x,y)$ is defined.\n\nAs a result, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined for all values of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the domain of $f$ is the entire $xy$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278213 :question/problem "Evaluate $z=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-1$ at the point $(3,{e}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(3,{e}^{2}),$ we substitute $x=3$ and $y={e}^{2}$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(3,{e}^{2})} & =(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-1){|}_{(3,{e}^{2})} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})-1 \\\\  & =3(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)-1 \\\\  & =3(2)-1 \\\\  & =6-1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278214 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\ne 0\\end{cases}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1\\end{cases}$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1\\}.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278215 :question/problem "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3{y}^{3}-x+2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(5,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(5,-1),$ we substitute $x=5$ and $y=-1$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5,-1) & =(3{y}^{3}-x+2){|}_{(5,-1)} \\\\  & =3(-1)^{3}-5+2 \\\\  & =-3-5+2 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278216 :question/problem "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{xy+7}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(-4,-5).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(-4,-5),$ we substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-5$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4,-5) & =\\sqrt[3]{xy+7}\\,{|}_{(-4,-5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(-4)(-5)+7} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{20+7} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278217 :question/problem "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(-6,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(-6,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-6,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,{|}_{(-6,-\\pi /4)} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-6\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278243 :question/problem "Find ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}y}-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}y}-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}y})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}y})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & ={e}^{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})-y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot y\\cdot 2x-y\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =2xy{e}^{{x}^{2}y}-\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278244 :question/problem "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2xy-1}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2xy-1}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2xy-1}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-1) & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{2\\sqrt{2xy-1}}(2x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{2\\sqrt{2xy-1}}\\cdot (2x\\cdot 1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{\\sqrt{2xy-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278245 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),$ evaluate ${f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},2).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot (2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot y\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =2x{y}^{2}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},2) & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}+2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2\\pi +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\pi +2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278246 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+y}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x+y}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\right) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(x+y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)}{\\operatorname{sin}^{2}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(x+y)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{\\operatorname{sin}^{2}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{\\operatorname{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(\\pi ,\\pi /2)} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-(\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-0}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278247 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x}{y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\cdot (y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{(y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(0,2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(0,2)} & =\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+0}{(2+0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278248 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{3x+y}{\\sqrt{x+y}}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{2(x+y)\\sqrt{x+y}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient and chain rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{3x+y}{\\sqrt{x+y}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{x+y}-(3x+y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x+y})}{{(\\sqrt{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3\\cdot \\sqrt{x+y}-(3x+y)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y))}{{(\\sqrt{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3\\cdot \\sqrt{x+y}-(3x+y)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot 1)}{{(\\sqrt{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{6(x+y)-(3x+y)}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\right)}{x+y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3x+5y}{2(x+y)\\sqrt{x+y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=3x+5y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278249 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2x{y}^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}y}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{2h(x,y)}{{x}^{3}y}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{2x{y}^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}y}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}-1)\\cdot {x}^{2}y-(2x{y}^{2}-1)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)}{({x}^{2}y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}y-(2x{y}^{2}-1)\\cdot 2xy}{{x}^{4}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2xy}{{x}^{4}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy-2{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{{x}^{4}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy(1-x{y}^{2})}{{x}^{4}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-x{y}^{2})}{{x}^{3}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=1-x{y}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278250 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ for $f(x,y)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x{y}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{y}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{y}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) & =(\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))-{y}^{3}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =((4{x}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{4}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))-{y}^{3} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278251 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-{y}^{2}}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{{(x-{y}^{2})}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot (x-{y}^{2})-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-{y}^{2})}{{(x-{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y\\cdot (x-{y}^{2})-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot (-2y)}{{(x-{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy-2{y}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+2{y}^{3}}{{(x-{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy+2{x}^{2}y}{{(x-{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy(1+x)}{{(x-{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=2xy(1+x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278254 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+x\\sqrt{y},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},4).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+x\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\sqrt{y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product, the chain, and the constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\sqrt{y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) & =(\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)))+\\sqrt{y}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy))+\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x))+\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1+\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(1/4,4)} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4)+1+\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278259 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ evaluate ${f}_{y}(2,\\sqrt{5}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain, the sum, and the constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) & =1\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+y\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot ({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot ({x}^{2}\\cdot 0+2y) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,\\sqrt{5}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\sqrt{5})} & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}}+\\frac{{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+5}+\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{4+5}} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278260 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(2,\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) & =x(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =x(1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi )} & =2\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(-1-\\pi \\cdot 0)+(-1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278287 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3u+2v \\\\ u+3v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3u+2v$ and $y(u,v)=u+3v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =9-2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278288 :question/problem "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ \\frac{v}{u}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=uv$ and $y=\\frac{v}{u}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & u \\\\ -\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{1}{u}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v\\cdot \\frac{1}{u}\\text{ }-u\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2v}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,3)}=\\frac{2(3)}{-2}=-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278291 :question/problem "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}(uv)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u-v}$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u-v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u-v} & -{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u-v}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)-(-{e}^{u-v})\\cdot \\frac{1}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u-v}}{v}+\\frac{{e}^{u-v}}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,1)}=\\frac{{e}^{(1)-(1)}}{(1)}+\\frac{{e}^{(1)-(1)}}{(1)}=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278292 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T(\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u+v}$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{v}-\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u+v}(u-v)}{vu}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278293 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T(\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ 4u+6v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)$ and $y=4u+6v.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\,\\text{ }4\\, & \\,\\text{ }6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\cdot 6-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v))\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278296 :question/problem "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2v \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-2v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-4uv-4uv \\\\  & =-8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,1)}=-8(-2)(1)=16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278297 :question/problem "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}{2} \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\frac{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}u & -v \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =u\\cdot u-(-v)\\cdot v \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,-2)}=(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278298 :question/problem "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{v}^{2}+uv \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={v}^{2}+uv$ and $y(u,v)=v-u.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v+u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & 2v+u \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-(2v+u)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =v+2v+u \\\\  & =3v+u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(8,-2)}=3(-2)+8=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278300 :question/problem "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}u \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡v-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{6},0)}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278358 :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa5b04b98d3796656fca912d8dc1732ea7e12bab9083f8ca543ad5fd4fdb10ff.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{e}^{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278359 :question/problem "![](images/cd/cdd5f0c58228b47a0bbba77daf2b426ce0247e71259e2b5af1a4a80fa833a036.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278360 :question/problem "Evaluate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }3r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}(\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{2}-0)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta (1-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[(\\operatorname{sin}\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{sin}0-\\frac{1}{3}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[(1-\\frac{1}{3})-0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278361 :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=15{x}^{2}y$ over the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/9b/9b54b7a488a1983ba1816fdf5c55e039951eb417102c61963977b2f48b9c8d9e.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}12{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}15({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}15{r}^{4}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{1}5{r}^{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}{[{r}^{5}]}_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )(1-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-({\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}(2\\pi )-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}(0)) \\\\  & =-(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278363 :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=4{y}^{2}$ over the disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/0a/0a1de805051eff4ea82b4eefb7bb113c724b3d4e7304704831b6294ee4e4d674.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}4(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{r}^{3}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}4{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}{[{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta (81-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =81{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =81{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}[(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}4\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278364 :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ over the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/81/811cfa127ad232e8d5a990998604718a57344d1bd7f43173dc0987631c13db33.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{e}^{(r{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{e}^{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.222em}{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{r}^{2}}]}_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\pi ({e}^{4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278365 :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ over the disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/0a/0a1de805051eff4ea82b4eefb7bb113c724b3d4e7304704831b6294ee4e4d674.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}2({(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}2{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.222em}{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{81}{4}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278366 :question/problem "![](images/71/719f86db69489f62bcbe2753e88cdc6713f04dc6f53415cc2484e2eaab8ffac3.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(0,1).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }(2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta -0)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278369 :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/f0/f0417011f46d3860b7de86fe652a7d80dd9787cb05c2057601b41c17e0c2b423.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -1)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/3f/3f4bb1428a6cc9b7e191e1904636d654145d9a1f2fb1dd40843d8cd878194772.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/d6/d6f4e054767650cd2a3d348aee8210af421e2df21792d79f42c5a58b5f4e82e6.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278370 :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/86/86036fd86096355b969da695f812c9bf835880edd68f7991183b70a6234e3eb4.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/9e/9ef22dcc15175664331d989bd50d9a8d60a354a0780dbaf9465caca04f28c2d9.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis) and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/f7/f724daf8321195815c9e413aab2106c611bc9d2e9653939266fc828aebeca68c.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{r}^{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278371 :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16,\\,y\\le 0\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/d8/d882bf70ecb7b94eee0eeeffbf63874ca8607268167f96278deb4678ec3b827a.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(4,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -4)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -8r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +16+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/60/605d9ce1c9030d1ac83c0a320bd648b42893f222279dd2d56b111acd9957c202.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis) and $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/f3/f37e34042338af2b1ba5f0c214ebf046eeba068b0b3238b662910a868c069ee4.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta }\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278374 :question/problem "![](images/4d/4da25a7fcce468774aafcf2c1dbf7b4ef65089194d0330b1159d18a9a8f4bfe9.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the plane is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{ }dA & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278379 :question/problem "![](images/ae/aed70cc2e5495fcfd9f42ff5d79c3371fb17b62e12ab1909c7738071e59f1a23.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2$ and the line $y=x+2,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(-1,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2 & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-2r(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ r(r-2(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).$\n\n- For $y=x+2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\ r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =2 \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-2,0)$ and $(0,2).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/b9/b9c2cd48302ef8d4e2d5e65a56d19506600c5e6552a4d9a1ef7d578a09a21557.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\le r\\le 2(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
